arXiv:2510.06750v1 [cs.CL] 08 Oct 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Gold-Switch: Training-Free Superposition of Slow- and Fast- Thinking LLMs</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Jaeseong Lee
</span><span id="id1" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_contribution ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span><span id="id1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">˜˜Equal contribution</span></span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Dayoung Kwon<span id="footnotex1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnotemark"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">footnotemark: </span>
          <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
          
          
          
        </span></span></span>
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Seung-won Hwang
</span><span id="id2" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_correspondence ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span><span id="id2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">˜˜Correspond to seungwonh@snu.ac.kr</span></span></span></span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Computer Science and Engineering, Seoul National University
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">{tbvj5914,ekdud1660,seungwonh}@snu.ac.kr</span></a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Large Reasoning Models (LRMs) excel in structured tasks by emulating deliberate human reasoning but often suffer from overthinking, degrading performance and wasting resources. One possible baseline is to deploy both LLM and LRM, then route input by predicting whether it requires reasoning and may cause overthinking.
However, deploying multiple models
can be costly or impractical. We propose a superposed deployment strategy with a lightweight, training-free regulation to optimize inference by switching one model on and off. Instead of routing, we selectively unlearn from LRM at inference, scaling down computation while preserving reasoning. By analyzing the cumulative energy of singular values, we identify optimal low-rank projections to adjust reasoning just right.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para ltx_align_bottom">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center" style="width:345.0pt;"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;"></span></p>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recently, large reasoning models (LRMs), such as DeepSeek-R1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">DeepSeek-AI et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, or QwQ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qwen, 2025</a>)</cite>, have emerged as a powerful class of reasoning-oriented models. These models, starting from a base large language model (LLM), then trained with extensive chain-of-thought (CoT) supervision, demonstrate strong performance across complex reasoning tasks such as mathematical problem solving, programming, and multi-step question answering.
LRMs exemplify a new generation of LLMs optimized for “slow thinking”– producing elaborate intermediate steps that mimic human reasoning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, overthinking <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sui et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cuadron et al., 2025</a>)</cite> has recently been identified as a critical challenge in LRMs: LRMs often generate unnecessarily long or convoluted reasoning paths <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sui et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
This behavior has been shown to reduce both efficiency and even lower the task performance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cuadron et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
This highlights the need to switch between slow- and fast- thinking in LRMs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">One possible solution is training a model to allow switching between slow- and fast- thinking by predefined prompt, such as Qwen3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. (2025)</a></cite>.
However due to their limited performance, the recent trend is back to releasing LRM and LLM separately– models such as Qwen3-2507, Qwen3-VL, Qwen3-Next <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. (2025)</a></cite>, Qwen3-Omni <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al. (2025a)</a></cite>, and Qwen3-Max <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team (2025)</a></cite> all trained and released slow- and fast- variants separately.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">An alternative solution
is deploying both LRM and LLM, and routing the prompts to the appropriate model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chuang et al., 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chuang et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Aytes et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ong et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. For example, RouteLLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ong et al., 2025</a>)</cite> trains a lightweight classifier to classify whether the input prompt is relatively hard or easy, then uses this classifier to route input prompts.
However, deployment of both LRM and LLM can be costly in practice.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we introduce a <span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">superposed deployment</span> strategy for achieving the “Goldilocks" balance between slow-thinking LRMs and fast-thinking LLMs.
One key inspiration is
that <span id="S1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">unlearning overthinking</span> from LRM
while preserving reasoning is possible. Specifically, for linear layers in LRM and LLM, denoted as <math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{R}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{B}</annotation></semantics></math>,
we hypothesize the existence of <math id="S1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L+W_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L+W_{R}</annotation></semantics></math> unlearns overthinking only.
In other words, instead of full difference
<math id="S1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta W=W_{B}-W_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta W=W_{B}-W_{R}</annotation></semantics></math>,
we can isolate an overthinking-specific
component <math id="S1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>, which is hypothesized to be low-rank, by low intrinsic dimensionality <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2018</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">With this formulation, our problem reduces
to finding <math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>: Considering both <math id="S1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta W</annotation></semantics></math> have similar direction– moving from <math id="S1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{R}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{B}</annotation></semantics></math>– we derive <math id="S1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> by low-rank approximating <math id="S1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta W</annotation></semantics></math> with rank <math id="S1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>.
To optimize <math id="S1.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>,
we introduce an entropy-based, layer-wise rank selection method inspired by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al. (2020)</a></cite>. We show that this layer-wise <math id="S1.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> selection achieves faster inference and higher task performance compared to naïvely using uniformly fixed ratio of <math id="S1.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> across every layer.
The key contributions of Gold-Switch are:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose Gold-Switch, a <span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">superposed deployment</span> of slow-thinking LRMs and fast-thinking LLMs with “Goldilocks" balance between them.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our key intuition is unlearning just right, or, avoiding overthinking while preserving reasoning, which we enable by low-rank approximating the parameter difference between LRM and LLM.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Experiments with QwQ-32B, DeepSeek-R1-Distill-32B, on ASDIV, GSM8K, AIME, and GPQA show that we draw LRM near the Goldilocks zone– we can get up to 2.7× speedup with negligible performance drop, while reducing the additional GPU memory by 9× compared with router-based solutions.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We release our code and models to the public,<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
                  
                  
                  
                https://anonymous.4open.science/r/GoldSwitch-377D/</span></span></span> enabling further research in this area.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Works</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Overthinking in LRMs</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">LRMs like DeepSeek-R1 and QwQ reportedly excel at complex multi-step tasks through chain-of-thought (CoT) training.
Meanwhile, they may generate long and repeated reasoning paths even for simple problems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sui et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cuadron et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2025)</a></cite> define overthinking as the inefficient use of computation, where models spend too much effort on simple tasks without much accuracy gain.
For example, they show that even for a simple question like “What is the answer of 2 plus 3?”, LRM such as QwQ-32B-Preview requires 901 tokens to reach the answer ‘5’, while an LLM from the same family, Qwen2.5-72B, requires only 7 tokens.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Reducing Overthinking</h3>

<section id="S2.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.2.1 </span>Training-Based</h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Reinforcement learning or supervised fine-tuning approaches, by rewarding concise, accurate answers and penalize long or incorrect ones, can encourage the model to generate shorter reasoning paths <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cui et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Munkhbat et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ma et al., 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kang et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shen et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Aggarwal and Welleck, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Arora and Zanette, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Luo et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yeo et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xia et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
For example, models like Kimi k1.5 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team et al., 2025</a>)</cite> apply length-aware rewards during their RL process to encourage shorter responses. It also utilizes rejection sampling, generating and evaluating candidate responses to guide efficient generation. Other methods create length-optimized datasets by removing redundant steps or tokens <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xia et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, or use self-training to sample shorter paths as pseudo-labels <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Munkhbat et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Models like Qwen3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. (2025)</a></cite> are trained to allow both slow- and fast- thinking by predefined prompts. However, due to their limited performance, the recent trend is back to releasing slow- and fast- thinking models separately <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al. (2025a)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team (2025)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS1.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Distinction</h5>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While these methods usually require extensive data- and compute- resources, Gold-Switch is training-free, removing such a burden.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.2.2 </span>Training-Free</h4>

<section id="S2.SS2.SSS2.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Routing-based</h5>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Routing-based method classifies the input prompt to route to an appropriate model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chuang et al., 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chuang et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Aytes et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ong et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. For example, RouteLLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ong et al., 2025</a>)</cite> trains a lightweight classifier to classify whether the input prompt is relatively hard or easy, then uses this classifier to route input prompts.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS2.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Prompt-based</h5>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Prompt-based approaches adjust the input format to limit reasoning steps <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Renze and Guven, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ma et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>. For example, minimal prompting strategies like Nothinking <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ma et al., 2025a</a>)</cite> encourage direct answers without intermediate steps, by early closure of &lt;think&gt; tag with &lt;/think&gt; (<a href="#A2.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ Effect of Thresholds ‣ Appendix B Ablation Study ‣ Gold-Switch: Training-Free Superposition of Slow- and Fast- Thinking LLMs" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 5</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS2.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Distinction</h5>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS2.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While the routing-based method requires deploying both LRM and LLM, we propose a superposed deployment strategy.
Moreover, our method is orthogonal to prompt-based methods, which can be combined to further enhance the efficiency.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>Low-Rank Representation</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning (PEFT) methods, especially LoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, have become a standard for adapting large language models (LLMs) to downstream tasks. These methods rely on the hypothesis that the parameter changes required to learn a specific task or capability have a much lower intrinsic dimension than the full parameter space <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Aghajanyan et al., 2021</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">MoDeGPT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al. (2025)</a></cite> modularizes model parameters by task and recombines them, demonstrating that LLMs can be compressed and reused efficiently without additional training.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Distinction</h5>

<div id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Gold-Switch demonstrates that unlearning reasoning capacities can also be captured in a compact, low-dimensional subspace without additional training.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Proposed Method</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Preliminaries</h3>

<section id="S3.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.1.1 </span>LRMs</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To enhance the reasoning capabilities of large language models (LLMs), researchers have developed various reasoning-oriented models, such as QwQ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qwen, 2025</a>)</cite> and DeepSeek-R1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">DeepSeek-AI et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. These models are typically trained on a base model (e.g., Qwen2.5-32B) with extensive chain-of-thought (CoT) supervision, enabling them to perform complex reasoning tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Formally, we denote the base model as <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{B}</annotation></semantics></math>.
By updating this model we obtain a reasoning-enhanced model <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{R}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{R}=W_{B}+\Delta W" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{R}=W_{B}+\Delta W</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta W</annotation></semantics></math> represents the parameter update needed for complex reasoning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">However, this reasoning-enhanced model often suffers from overthinking, where it generates unnecessarily long or convoluted reasoning paths <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sui et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. This behavior can lead to inefficiencies and even degradation of task performance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cuadron et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.1.2 </span>Baseline: Routing Input Queries to LRM and LLMs</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To avoid overthinking, we can adopt RouteLLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ong et al., 2025</a>)</cite> to route the input query to the appropriate model.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Formally, given a lightweight classifier <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> that predicts the difficulty of the input, RouteLLM routes the input <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{R}</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(x)\leq t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(x)\leq t</annotation></semantics></math>, and to <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{B}</annotation></semantics></math> otherwise.
In theory, the classifier <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> can be either black-boxed, or a BERT-based classifier trained on a preference dataset.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">However, this solution blindly deploys both <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{R}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{B}</annotation></semantics></math>, which can be costly in practice.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.06750v1/Figur1.png" id="S3.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:381/148;" width="381" height="148" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>
Overview of Gold-Switch. Gold-Switch extracts the overthinking parameters from the reasoning-enhanced model using low-rank approximation. It then dynamically adjusts the reasoning capabilities based on the difficulty of the input, allowing for efficient reasoning without retraining.
</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Gold-Switch: Training-Free Superposition Between LRM and LLM</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3.1.2 Baseline: Routing Input Queries to LRM and LLMs ‣ 3.1 Preliminaries ‣ 3 Proposed Method ‣ Gold-Switch: Training-Free Superposition of Slow- and Fast- Thinking LLMs" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 1</span></a> illustrates the overall flow of Gold-Switch.
We start by defining <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>, for unlearning only overthinking from LRM (§<a href="#S3.SS2.SSS1" title="3.2.1 Training-Free 𝐿 to Unlearn Only Overthinking from LRM ‣ 3.2 Gold-Switch: Training-Free Superposition Between LRM and LLM ‣ 3 Proposed Method ‣ Gold-Switch: Training-Free Superposition of Slow- and Fast- Thinking LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2.1</span></a>).
This lightweight module could be deployed alongside <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, with minimal overhead. Then we introduce how to superpose <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> with the given LRM, for each given query (§<a href="#S3.SS2.SSS2" title="3.2.2 Hard and Soft Superposition ‣ 3.2 Gold-Switch: Training-Free Superposition Between LRM and LLM ‣ 3 Proposed Method ‣ Gold-Switch: Training-Free Superposition of Slow- and Fast- Thinking LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2.2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.1 </span>Training-Free <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> to Unlearn Only Overthinking from LRM</h4>

<section id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph"><math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> as a low-rank approximation of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta{W}</annotation></semantics></math></h5>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The key intuition to construct <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is that <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">unlearning overthinking</span> while preserving reasoning is possible. In other words, unlike <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta W=W_{B}-W_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta W=W_{B}-W_{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, which captures all finetuned capabilities including reasoning, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> focuses solely on overthinking, implying lower intrinsic dimensionality than <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta W</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2018</a>)</cite>.
With low intrinsic dimensionality, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> could be low-rank decomposed, enabling lightweight deployment alongside <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{R}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our second key intuition is that <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta W</annotation></semantics></math> have similar direction, both shifting <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{R}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{B}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Therefore we hypothesize that <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> can be a low-rank approximation of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta W</annotation></semantics></math>.
Our experiments (Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Experiments ‣ Gold-Switch: Training-Free Superposition of Slow- and Fast- Thinking LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) will reveal that when we low-rank approximate the full unlearning matrix <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta W</annotation></semantics></math>, slow-thinking unlearning is captured first in these low-rank approximations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Formally, we decompose <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta{W}</annotation></semantics></math> as :</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta W\approx USV^{T}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta W\approx USV^{T}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times\min(N,M)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times\min(N,M)}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\in\mathbb{R}^{\min(N,M)\times\min(N,M)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\in\mathbb{R}^{\min(N,M)\times\min(N,M)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\in\mathbb{R}^{M\times\min(N,M)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\in\mathbb{R}^{M\times\min(N,M)}</annotation></semantics></math>. Following the Eckart-Young-Mirsky theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Eckart and Young, 1936</a>)</cite>, we can approximate <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta W</annotation></semantics></math> with a low-rank matrix by selecting the top <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> singular values from <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.
This allows us to select the essential dimensions of the delta matrix <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta W</annotation></semantics></math>.
Formally, we get the low-rank approximation of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta W</annotation></semantics></math> as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\hat{U}=U[:,:r],\quad\hat{S}=S[:r,:r],\quad\hat{V}=V[:,:r]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>U</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo>,</mo><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>S</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo>,</mo><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{U}=U[:,:r],\quad\hat{S}=S[:r,:r],\quad\hat{V}=V[:,:r]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, we get the Gold-Switch <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=L_{U}\cdot L_{V},\quad L_{U}=\hat{U}\cdot\sqrt{\hat{S}},\quad L_{V}=\sqrt{\hat{S}}\cdot\hat{V}^{T}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>V</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msqrt><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msqrt><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msqrt><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=L_{U}\cdot L_{V},\quad L_{U}=\hat{U}\cdot\sqrt{\hat{S}},\quad L_{V}=\sqrt{\hat{S}}\cdot\hat{V}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">The resulting <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> can selectively remove unnecessary overthinking components while preserving core reasoning.
This approach is crucial for retaining the reasoning of large LRMs while enhancing efficiency during inference.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Energy-based <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> selection</h5>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Now, the problem narrows down to how to effectively select <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> for each layer. A blind selection of a high rank would make <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> too similar to <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta W</annotation></semantics></math>, causing the unlearning process to strip away not only overthinking components but also critical reasoning abilities.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To address this, inspired by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al. (2020)</a></cite>, we employ an energy-based selection mechanism driven by the energy percentage <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math>. According to matrix perturbation theory <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Stewart and Sun, 1990</a>)</cite>, the difference between two matrices is bounded by the differences in their singular values, as expressed by the inequality:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|B-A\|_{F}^{2}\geq\sqrt{\sum_{i}|s_{b,i}-s_{a,i}|^{2}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>≥</mo><msqrt><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>i</mi></munder><msup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|B-A\|_{F}^{2}\geq\sqrt{\sum_{i}|s_{b,i}-s_{a,i}|^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{b,i},s_{a,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{b,i},s_{a,i}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the singular values of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> respectively. Applying this to our setting, we derive the lower bound of the error between <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta W</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|L-\Delta W\|_{F}^{2}\geq\sqrt{\sum_{i=r}^{\min(N,M)}s_{i}^{2}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>≥</mo><msqrt><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></munderover><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|L-\Delta W\|_{F}^{2}\geq\sqrt{\sum_{i=r}^{\min(N,M)}s_{i}^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the singular values of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta W</annotation></semantics></math>.
This lower bound serves as an indicator to prevent <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> from becoming too close to <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta W</annotation></semantics></math>, ensuring that essential reasoning is not inadvertently discarded. For each layer, we determine the optimal <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> by regulating the right-hand term of <a href="#S3.E6" title="6 ‣ Energy-based 𝑟 selection ‣ 3.2.1 Training-Free 𝐿 to Unlearn Only Overthinking from LRM ‣ 3.2 Gold-Switch: Training-Free Superposition Between LRM and LLM ‣ 3 Proposed Method ‣ Gold-Switch: Training-Free Superposition of Slow- and Fast- Thinking LLMs" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation 6</span></a>, preventing it from diminishing excessively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To this end, we introduce the reversed cumulative energy <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e(r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e(r)</annotation></semantics></math>, defined as follows:</p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e(r)=\frac{\sum_{i=r}^{\min(N,M)}s_{i}^{2}}{\sum_{i=1}^{\min(N,M)}s_{i}^{2}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e(r)=\frac{\sum_{i=r}^{\min(N,M)}s_{i}^{2}}{\sum_{i=1}^{\min(N,M)}s_{i}^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">We then select <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> as the minimum rank that maintains <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e(r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e(r)</annotation></semantics></math> above an empirically tuned threshold <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>. This ensures sufficient information retention while eliminating unnecessary overthinking components.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.2 </span>Hard and Soft Superposition</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Now the remaining part is how to superpose slow- and fast-thinking LRM with <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{R}</annotation></semantics></math>. We introduce hard or soft superposition.</p>
</div>
