<section id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Hard Superposition</h5>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a classifier <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> that predicts the difficulty of the input (§<a href="#S3.SS1.SSS2" title="3.1.2 Baseline: Routing Input Queries to LRM and LLMs ‣ 3.1 Preliminaries ‣ 3 Proposed Method ‣ Gold-Switch: Training-Free Superposition of Slow- and Fast- Thinking LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1.2</span></a>),
we can switch between slow-thinking and fast-thinking based on the predicted difficulty:</p>
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{\prime}_{R}=\begin{cases}W_{R}+L&amp;\text{if }C(x)\leq t\\
W_{R}&amp;\text{if }C(x)&lt;t\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>R</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{\prime}_{R}=\begin{cases}W_{R}+L&amp;\text{if }C(x)\leq t\\
W_{R}&amp;\text{if }C(x)&lt;t\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{\prime}_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>R</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{\prime}_{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is the weight we use for the inference of input <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is a threshold that determines whether the input is considered easy or hard.
This enables us to deal with the complex tasks that require sophisticated reasoning capabilities via slow-thinking, while still benefiting from the reduced overthinking for easier tasks via fast-thinking.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Soft Superposition</h5>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Moreover, we investigate soft superposition to continuously adjust the weight of Gold-Switch based on the difficulty of the input, by generalizing the concept of routing in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ong et al. (2025)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Formally, the softly superposed <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{\prime}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{\prime}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as:</p>
<table id="S3.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{\prime}_{r}=W_{R}+\alpha L" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{\prime}_{r}=W_{R}+\alpha L</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is a scaling factor that controls the strength of Gold-Switch.
However, we find that scaling Gold-Switch may increase the overthinking in some cases, making it harder to control the reasoning capabilities (<a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.6 Case Study ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Gold-Switch: Training-Free Superposition of Slow- and Fast- Thinking LLMs" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 3</span></a>). Therefore we use the hard superposition for the final experiments.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we aim to address the following research questions:</p>
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">RQ1: Does Gold-Switch effectively reduce overthinking in LRMs?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">RQ2: Does Gold-Switch effectively retain the complex reasoning capability of LRMs?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">RQ3: Is Gold-Switch cost-effective to build and serve?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">RQ4: Is the entropy-based rank selection method effective?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">RQ5: How does soft switching compare to hard switching?</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
