</figure>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Models</h5>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We employ three open-sourced LRMs: QwQ-32B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qwen, 2025</a>)</cite>, DeekSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-32B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">DeepSeek-AI et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, and Qwen3-4B-Thinking-2507 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. (2025)</a></cite>.
As the base LLMs, we utilize Qwen2.5-32B-Instruct, Qwen2.5-32B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qwen et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, and Qwen3-4B-Instruct-2507, respectively.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Tasks and Datasets</h5>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We mainly evaluate with ASDIV <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Miao et al., 2020</a>)</cite> and GSM8K <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cobbe et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, where overthinking is reported to happen <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. As challenging reasoning problems where slow thinking is favored, we employ AIME2024. We also include GPQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rein et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, the non-math task <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2025)</a></cite> employed. All evaluations are done in a 0-shot manner.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Implementation Details</h5>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For AIME2024 and GPQA, we employ <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">open-r1</span>.<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
              
              
              
            github.com/huggingface/open-r1</span></span></span> To evaluate ASDIV and GSM8K, we extend <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">lm-evaluation-harness<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note"><span id="footnote3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">3</span></span>
                
                
                
              <span id="footnote3.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">github.com/neuralmagic/lm-evaluation-harness/tree/llama_3.1_instruct</span></span></span></span></span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al., 2021</a>)</cite> to use the suggested format by DeepSeek and Qwen. Following the suggestion, we use temperature of 0.6, topP of 0.95, topK of 30, and minP of 0.
For Qwen3 models, we use topK of 20, and for Qwen3-4B-Instruct-2507 and the non-thinking mode of Qwen3-4B, we use temperature of 0.7, topP of 0.8.
We use max new tokens of 32K, while
for ASDIV and GSM8K evaluation of DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-32B and Qwen2.5-32B, we use max new tokens of 2K, to align with the default configuration of huggingface Qwen2.5-32B. We use <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=0.78" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.78</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=0.78</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=0.6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=0.6</annotation></semantics></math>.<span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">4</span>
              
              
              
            We conducted small grid search once on QwQ-32B with 100 samples of GSM8K to choose this value, and used over all models and tasks, as elaborated in Appendix B.</span></span></span>
For classificer <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, we use a pretrained BERT-based model introduced in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ong et al. (2025)</a></cite>, which is claimed to generalize over diverse settings.
All experiments are conducted on 2×A6000.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Comparisons</h5>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compare the following methods:</p>
<ul id="S4.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LRM</span> breaks down structured problems into multiple steps for deeper understanding but often overthink even on simple tasks.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LLM</span> can produce concise responses with low computation, but often lacks deep logical reasoning for complex problems.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RouteLLM</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ong et al., 2025</a>)</cite> selects different language models (such as LLM or LRM) depending on the difficulty of the input. For simple inputs, it chooses a lightweight LLM, and for harder problems, it uses an LRM. In our setup, we use a BERT-based router with a confidence threshold to decide which model to use, which we use in Gold-Switch as well.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Nothinking</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ma et al., 2025a</a>)</cite> skips the reasoning process by pre-filling an assistant message with ’&lt;/think&gt;’ without training. It also uses a Token Budget (set to 3500 in our tests) to force short responses, reducing overthinking.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Gold-Switch</span> is our proposal. We define the three methods as follows.</p>
<ul id="S4.I2.i5.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I2.i5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S4.I2.i5.I1.i1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i5.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Always On</span> applies unlearning reasoning to all queries without distinguishing task difficulty.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S4.I2.i5.I1.i2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i5.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hard Switch</span> completely enables or disables unlearning based on a query classifier <math id="S4.I2.i5.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i5.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S4.I2.i5.I1.i3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i5.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i5.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i5.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Soft Switch</span> adjusts unlearning based on query difficulty, providing continuous control.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
