</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>RQ2: Gold-Switch Effectively Retains the Complex Reasoning Capability of LRMs</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ Comparisons ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Gold-Switch: Training-Free Superposition of Slow- and Fast- Thinking LLMs" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 4</span></a>, <a href="#S4.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ Comparisons ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Gold-Switch: Training-Free Superposition of Slow- and Fast- Thinking LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> and <a href="#S4.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ 4.1 RQ1: Gold-Switch Effectively Reduces Overthinking in LRMs ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Gold-Switch: Training-Free Superposition of Slow- and Fast- Thinking LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> present the results of Gold-Switch on AIME2024 and GPQA with QwQ-32B, DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-32B, and Qwen3-4B, respectively. While always switching on Gold-Switch effectively reduces overthinking and preserves performance in easy tasks, it fails to do so in complex tasks. For instance, QwQ-32B achieves 66.7% accuracy on AIME2024, whereas always enabling Gold-Switch drops it to 16.7%. In contrast, Gold-Switch with its switching mechanism retains QwQ-32B’s reasoning capability even in complex scenarios, highlighting its necessity.
The trend on GPQA is consistent as well.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>RQ3: Gold-Switch Is Cost-Efficient to Build and Serve</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S4.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ 4.1 RQ1: Gold-Switch Effectively Reduces Overthinking in LRMs ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Gold-Switch: Training-Free Superposition of Slow- and Fast- Thinking LLMs" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 7</span></a> shows that the building cost of Gold-Switch is negligible– this training-free preprocessing step requires less than 1 hour with only 4GB of GPU memory.
<a href="#S4.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ 4.1 RQ1: Gold-Switch Effectively Reduces Overthinking in LRMs ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Gold-Switch: Training-Free Superposition of Slow- and Fast- Thinking LLMs" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 8</span></a> shows that the routing time is negligible as well. Even if we consider the time-to-first-token latency, routing time takes only 0.04×.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, the ‘Relative Size’ column from <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.2.2 Hard and Soft Superposition ‣ 3.2 Gold-Switch: Training-Free Superposition Between LRM and LLM ‣ 3 Proposed Method ‣ Gold-Switch: Training-Free Superposition of Slow- and Fast- Thinking LLMs" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 1</span></a>, <a href="#S3.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 3.2.2 Hard and Soft Superposition ‣ 3.2 Gold-Switch: Training-Free Superposition Between LRM and LLM ‣ 3 Proposed Method ‣ Gold-Switch: Training-Free Superposition of Slow- and Fast- Thinking LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Gold-Switch: Training-Free Superposition of Slow- and Fast- Thinking LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> shows that Gold-Switch significantly saves the deployment cost. While the routing-based solution, RouteLLM additionally requires 1× compared with serving one LRM, Gold-Switch only requires around 0.11×, which is 9× smaller.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>RQ4: Entropy-Based Rank Selection Is Indeed Better</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S4.T9" title="Table 9 ‣ 4.1 RQ1: Gold-Switch Effectively Reduces Overthinking in LRMs ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Gold-Switch: Training-Free Superposition of Slow- and Fast- Thinking LLMs" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 9</span></a>shows the performance of Gold-Switch with different rank selection methods on QwQ-32B.
We can observe that entropy-based rank selection can obtain not only a smaller size compared to the fixed ratio method, but also achieves better performance.
This emphasizes the importance of our layer-wise energy-based method to keep distance from <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta W</annotation></semantics></math> (Equation <a href="#S3.E6" title="In Energy-based 𝑟 selection ‣ 3.2.1 Training-Free 𝐿 to Unlearn Only Overthinking from LRM ‣ 3.2 Gold-Switch: Training-Free Superposition Between LRM and LLM ‣ 3 Proposed Method ‣ Gold-Switch: Training-Free Superposition of Slow- and Fast- Thinking LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.5 </span>RQ5: Soft Switching Fails to Reduce Overthinking</h3>

<div id="S4.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.6 Case Study ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Gold-Switch: Training-Free Superposition of Slow- and Fast- Thinking LLMs" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 3</span></a>illustrates the relative average token length ratio of hard and soft superposition compared to LRM (DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-32B), using 90 samples from ASDIV. As <math id="S4.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> varies between 0 and 1, the average token length of output from <math id="S4.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{R}+\alpha L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{R}+\alpha L</annotation></semantics></math> may increase, suggesting that the soft switching mechanism is ineffective in mitigating overthinking. In contrast, for easy problems, hard superposition effectively reduces token length.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.6 </span>Case Study</h3>

<div id="S4.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#A2.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Effect of Thresholds ‣ Appendix B Ablation Study ‣ Gold-Switch: Training-Free Superposition of Slow- and Fast- Thinking LLMs" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 4</span></a>, <a href="#A2.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ Effect of Thresholds ‣ Appendix B Ablation Study ‣ Gold-Switch: Training-Free Superposition of Slow- and Fast- Thinking LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> and <a href="#A2.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ Effect of Thresholds ‣ Appendix B Ablation Study ‣ Gold-Switch: Training-Free Superposition of Slow- and Fast- Thinking LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> in the Appendix show a case study for the same question
“Sophia and Rose went together to the market to buy onions and potatoes. Rose bought 4 times the number of onions and potatoes Sophia bought. If Rose bought 12 onions and 4 potatoes, how many onions and potatoes in total did Sophia buy at the market?”.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">First, the base LRM, QwQ-32B, overthinks over and over again– repeatedly obtaining the same answer “4” for 10 times– resulting in 1353 tokens to reach the final answer.
NoThinking <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ma et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>, by closing the &lt;/think&gt; beforehand, reduces the thinking process, but fails to avoid repeated answer of “4” for 6 times, resulting in 984 tokens to reach the final answer.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast, Gold-Switch directly dives into a single CoT process, without any repetitive answering, resulting in 328 tokens, almost 4× reduction of token length compared to the base LRM. Moreover, it is interesting to observe that the LRM seems to forget the &lt;think&gt; token, not reacting at all, even does not close with &lt;/think&gt; token.
The output token length is almost similar to the fast-thinking LLM, Qwen2.5-32B-Instruct (<a href="#A2.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ Effect of Thresholds ‣ Appendix B Ablation Study ‣ Gold-Switch: Training-Free Superposition of Slow- and Fast- Thinking LLMs" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 7</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.06750v1/ratio.svg" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/358;" width="476" height="358"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Average token length ratio of ASDIV on DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-32B</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our work
introduces lightweight, training-free LoRA modules that suppress overthinking, enabling LLMs to dynamically modulate their inference budget based on input complexity.
This approach not only avoids the need for deploying two models, but also makes inference more efficient.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Limitations</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Gold-Switch leverages classifiers to route hard or easy tasks from existing baselines <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ong et al. (2025)</a></cite>. Therefore we follow the limitations from those baselines, such as interpretability, or scarcity of gold-labeled dataset to train such classifiers.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Gold-Switch assumes access to both the base and fine-tuned weights, which may not be feasible for closed-source or commercial LLMs. However, our target is making local deployment more efficient with superposed deployment, thus supporting the closed-source models is out of our scope.</p>
</div>
</section>
