<td id="A2.T12.2.3.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b">92%</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 12: </span>Comparison on GSM8K with Qwen3-4B </figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Table <a href="#A2.T12" title="Table 12 ‣ Effect of Thresholds ‣ Appendix B Ablation Study ‣ Gold-Switch: Training-Free Superposition of Slow- and Fast- Thinking LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>, we empirically selected the best hyperparameters based on performance across a small grid search.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.F4" class="ltx_figure">
<p id="A2.F4.2" class="ltx_p">

<span id="A2.F4.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle ltx_framed ltx_framed_rectangle" style="width:345.0pt;">
<span id="A2.F4.2.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.F4.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF8000;">&lt;think&gt;</span>
Okay, let’s see. So the problem is about Sophia and Rose buying onions and potatoes at the market. Rose bought 4 times the number of onions and potatoes that Sophia bought. And we know Rose bought 12 onions and 4 potatoes. The question is asking how many onions and potatoes Sophia bought in total.</span>
<span id="A2.F4.2.1.2" class="ltx_p">Hmm, first, <span id="A2.F4.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">let me parse the problem again.</span> (…omitted…)
Therefore, Sophia bought 3 onions and 1 potato. <span id="A2.F4.2.1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The total would be 3 + 1 = 4</span>.</span>
<span id="A2.F4.2.1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.F4.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Wait, but let me make sure I didn’t misinterpret the problem</span> (…omitted…)</span>
<span id="A2.F4.2.1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.F4.2.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Alternatively,</span> (…omitted…) <span id="A2.F4.2.1.4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Alternatively,</span> (…omitted…)
Therefore, <span id="A2.F4.2.1.4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">total for Sophia is 3 + 1 = 4</span>.</span>
<span id="A2.F4.2.1.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.F4.2.1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Alternatively,</span> (…omitted…) <span id="A2.F4.2.1.5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Sophia’s total is 4</span>.</span>
<span id="A2.F4.2.1.6" class="ltx_p">Hmm, so either way, whether we take it per item or combined, the answer comes out the same? Wait, that’s interesting. <span id="A2.F4.2.1.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Let me check both interpretations again.</span> (…omitted…)</span>
<span id="A2.F4.2.1.7" class="ltx_p">So either way, <span id="A2.F4.2.1.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">the answer is 4</span>. <span id="A2.F4.2.1.7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Wait,</span> (…omitted…) Therefore, <span id="A2.F4.2.1.7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">the answer is 4</span>.</span>
<span id="A2.F4.2.1.8" class="ltx_p">Therefore, I think <span id="A2.F4.2.1.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">the answer is 4</span>. <span id="A2.F4.2.1.8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Let me just confirm once again.</span> (…omitted…)
So Sophia’s <span id="A2.F4.2.1.8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">total is 4</span>.</span>
<span id="A2.F4.2.1.9" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.F4.2.1.9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Alternatively,</span> (…omitted…)
Therefore, Sophia bought 3 onions and 1 potato, <span id="A2.F4.2.1.9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">totaling 4. </span></span>
<span id="A2.F4.2.1.10" class="ltx_p">I think that’s solid. So <span id="A2.F4.2.1.10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">the answer should be 4.</span>
<span id="A2.F4.2.1.10.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF8000;">&lt;/think&gt;</span></span>
<span id="A2.F4.2.1.11" class="ltx_p">Sophia bought onions and potatoes such that Rose, who purchased 4 times as many of each, ended up with 12 onions and 4 potatoes. Let <math id="A2.F4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>O</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O</annotation></semantics></math> be the number of onions and <math id="A2.F4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> the number of potatoes Sophia bought. Since Rose bought 4 times as many:</span>
<span id="A2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<span><span class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<span class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></span>
<span class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4O=12\implies O=\frac{12}{4}=3" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>12</mn><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mi>O</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>12</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4O=12\implies O=\frac{12}{4}=3</annotation></semantics></math></span>
<span class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></span></span></span>
</span>
<span id="A2.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<span><span class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<span class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></span>
<span class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4P=4\implies P=\frac{4}{4}=1" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>4</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4P=4\implies P=\frac{4}{4}=1</annotation></semantics></math></span>
<span class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></span></span></span>
</span>
<span id="A2.F4.2.1.12" class="ltx_p">Thus, Sophia bought <math id="A2.F4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3+1=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3+1=4</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.F4.2.1.12.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> items in total.</span>
<math id="A2.F4.m4" class="ltx_Math ltx_framed ltx_framed_rectangle" display="block" intent=":literal"><mn>4</mn></math> (1353 tokens)</span>
</span></p>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>Case study of QwQ</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A2.F5" class="ltx_figure">
<p id="A2.F5.2" class="ltx_p">

<span id="A2.F5.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle ltx_framed ltx_framed_rectangle" style="width:345.0pt;">
<span id="A2.F5.2.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.F5.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF8000;">&lt;think&gt;</span>
Okay I have finished thinking.<span id="A2.F5.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF8000;">&lt;/think&gt;</span></span>
<span id="A2.F5.2.1.2" class="ltx_p">Alright, let’s tackle this problem step by step. So Sophia and Rose went to the market to buy onions and potatoes. The problem says that Rose bought 4 times the number of onions and potatoes that Sophia bought. Then it tells us that Rose bought 12 onions and 4 potatoes. We need to find out how many onions and potatoes Sophia bought in total. Hmm, let me break this down. (…omitted…)</span>
<span id="A2.F5.2.1.3" class="ltx_p">Adding those together, the <span id="A2.F5.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">total would be 3 + 1 = 4. </span></span>
<span id="A2.F5.2.1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.F5.2.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Wait, let me check that again to make sure I didn’t make a mistake.</span> (…omitted…)
Adding those together, <span id="A2.F5.2.1.4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">3 + 1 is indeed 4</span>. That seems straightforward.</span>
<span id="A2.F5.2.1.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.F5.2.1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Is there another way to approach this? Maybe by setting up equations.</span> (…omitted…)
<span id="A2.F5.2.1.5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Total for Sophia is O_s + P_s = 3 +1 =4</span>. Yep, same answer.</span>
<span id="A2.F5.2.1.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.F5.2.1.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hmm, maybe I should check if there’s any possibility that</span> (…omitted…) the <span id="A2.F5.2.1.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">final total for Sophia ends up being 4</span>.</span>
<span id="A2.F5.2.1.7" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.F5.2.1.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">But the problem says</span> "4 times the number of onions and potatoes", which is a bit ambiguous. (…omitted…)</span>
<span id="A2.F5.2.1.8" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.F5.2.1.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Alternatively,</span> (…omitted…)
So either way, the answer comes out the same.</span>
<span id="A2.F5.2.1.9" class="ltx_p">Therefore, regardless of the interpretation, the total number of onions and potatoes <span id="A2.F5.2.1.9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Sophia bought is 4</span>.</span>
<span id="A2.F5.2.1.10" class="ltx_p">So the <span id="A2.F5.2.1.10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">final answer should be 4</span>. I think that’s solid. Both interpretations lead to the same result, so there’s no ambiguity here.</span>
<span id="A2.F5.2.1.11" class="ltx_p">**Final Answer**
The total number of onions and potatoes <span id="A2.F5.2.1.11.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Sophia bought is <math id="A2.F5.m1" class="ltx_Math ltx_framed ltx_framed_rectangle" display="block" intent=":literal"><mn>4</mn></math></span>. (984 tokens)</span>
</span></p>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>Case study of QwQ+Nothinking</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A2.F6" class="ltx_figure">
<p id="A2.F6.2" class="ltx_p">

<span id="A2.F6.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle ltx_framed ltx_framed_rectangle" style="width:345.0pt;">
<span id="A2.F6.2.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.F6.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF8000;">&lt;think&gt;</span>
Let’s break down the problem step by step. (…omitted…)
Therefore, the total number of onions and potatoes that Sophia bought is <math id="A2.F6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boxed{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><menclose notation="box"><mn>4</mn></menclose><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boxed{4}</annotation></semantics></math>. (328 tokens)</span>
</span></p>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span>Case study of QwQ+Gold-Switch </figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A2.F7" class="ltx_figure">
<p id="A2.F7.2" class="ltx_p">

<span id="A2.F7.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle ltx_framed ltx_framed_rectangle" style="width:345.0pt;">
<span id="A2.F7.2.1.1" class="ltx_p">To find out how many onions and potatoes Sophia bought, (…omitted…) Therefore, Sophia bought a total of 4 items (onions and potatoes) at the market. (273 tokens)</span>
</span></p>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 7: </span>Case study of Qwen2.5-32B-Instruct</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
